{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"totalTokens":0,"cost":{"input":0,"output":0,"cacheRead":0,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"toolcall_delta","contentIndex":4,"delta":"{\"path\": \"benchmark/fixtures/T10-event-projector/INCIDENT.md\", \"content\": \"# Event projector incident\\n\\nStatus: resolved\\n\\n## Impact\\n\\nAfter a temporary persistence rejection, the projector failed every\\nsubsequent `project()` call for any trade (the shared promise chain stayed\\nrejected forever). Additionally, all trades were serialized behind a single\\nglobal queue, so one slow persistence blocked unrelated trades. State was\\nalso committed to the in-memory map before `applyToStore` succeeded, so a\\nfailed write left the snapshot ahead of the store. No versioning, duplicate,\\ngap, terminal-state, or payload validation existed.\\n\\n## Root cause\\n\\n`createEventProjector` chained every event onto one module-scope `pending`\\npromise (`pending = pending.then(...)`). Once `applyToStore` rejected, the\\nrejection propagated into `pending`, so all later events — for that trade or\\nany other — rejected immediately. The same global chain also prevented\\nconcurrent progress across trades. The reducer result was written to\\n`states` before awaiting persistence, committing uncommitted data on\\nfailure, and `reduceEvent` performed no version, lifecycle, or payload\\nchecks.\\n\\n## Fix\\n\\n- Replaced the single global queue with a per-trade promise tail, so events\\n  for one trade apply serially in call order while different trades proceed\\n  concurrently.\\n- Each tail is continued with a swallowing handler (`result.then(noop,\\n  noop)`), so a rejection of one event never poisons later work for that\\n  trade.\\n- `applyToStore` is awaited before `states.set`, so state commits only after\\n  persistence succeeds.\\n- Added validation: malformed events (bad shape, unknown type, bad payload)\\n  reject with `TypeError`; the first accepted event must be `CREATED` at\\n  version 1; later events must be exactly `version + 1`; duplicate/stale\\n  versions return the current snapshot without persisting; a `CANCELLED`\\n  trade is terminal and rejects any later new version; `CREATED` requires a\\n  positive finite `notional` and non-empty `owner`, and `AMENDED` must leave\\n  a valid complete state.\\n- All inputs, callback arguments, returned states, and snapshots are\\n  deep-cloned (`structuredClone`) so no mutable internal state escapes.\\n\\n## Regression coverage\\n\\n`student-tests.mjs` contains six tests:\\n\\n1. Different trades progress concurrently while one trade is blocked behind\\n   a held persistence promise.\\n2. Duplicate/stale versions return the current snapshot without persisting.\\n3. Version gaps and invalid first events reject without changing state, and\\n   the trade still accepts a valid follow-up.\\n4. A cancelled trade is terminal and rejects later new versions.\\n5. Malformed events reject with `TypeError`.\\n6. Inputs, store-callback values, returned states, and snapshots do not\\n   expose mutable internal state.\\n\\n## Remaining risk\\n\\nPer-trade tails are retained in a `Map` for the lifetime of the projector;\\na very large number of distinct trade IDs would grow that map (entries are\\nonly settled promises, so impact is small). Persistence failures surface to\\nthe caller but are not retried, by design — callers must resubmit the event.\\n\"}"}}
{"type":"message_update","usage":{"input":892,"output":3773,"cacheRead":3072,"cacheWrite":0,"reasoning":509,"totalTokens":7737,"cost":{"input":0.002676,"output":0.056595,"cacheRead":0.0009216,"cacheWrite":0,"total":0.0601926}},"assistantMessageEvent":{"type":"thinking_end","contentIndex":0,"content":"Note the read of event-projector.mjs output seems garbled (the reduceEvent CANCELLED part appeared inside createEventProjector text — actually the read output interleaved). The CANCELLED block likely is part of reduceEvent before the export. Anyway I'm rewriting the file fully.\n\nDesign:\n- Per-trade promise queue (map of tradeId -> tail promise).\n- Validation: event must be object with valid tradeId, finite version number, type in set, payload valid. Malformed → TypeError reject.\n- Version rules: first event must be CREATED at version 1, otherwise reject (Error). Later events: version === current.version+1, else if version <= current.version → return snapshot (duplicate/stale, no persist). Cancelled trade terminal → reject new version.\n- Commit state only after applyToStore succeeds.\n- Validation before enqueue? Errors should reject without changing state. If we validate inside the queue, a rejection of queue tail would poison subsequent per-trade events — so catch and keep tail going. Approach: per-trade tail = tail.then(run, run)? Better: validate synchronously-ish and only enqueue valid events; errors from applyToStore must not break the chain. Use `tail = tail.then(process)` and return a promise that rejects independently: actually if applyToStore rejects, tail becomes rejected; next event chained off it via .then would never run. Fix: `tails.set(id, tails.get(id).then(run, run))` — but then the returned promise should be the run result. Alternative: `const result = tails.get(id).then(run); tails.set(id, result.catch(() => {})); return result;`\n\nValidation errors and version-gap errors: \"rejects without changing state\" — these can be rejected immediately without touching queue. But ordering: validate inside run to keep serial semantics? Duplicate/stale check needs current state at run time. Do checks inside run, before persistence; the commit only after success.\n\nDeep-freeze? Not required; just clone inputs and outputs. Clone event at entry so caller mutation doesn't affect processing.\n\nCREATED payload validation: positive finite notional, non-empty owner. AMENDED may replace either but result must remain valid: notional positive finite, owner non-empty string.\n\nMalformed events → TypeError: non-object event, missing/invalid tradeId, unsupported type, non-integer/invalid version, missing payload for CREATED/AMENDED. Version gaps/invalid first → regular Error (reject).\n\nWrite code."}}
